(window.MUSEUM_PAINT_T = window.MUSEUM_PAINT_T || {})["en"] = {"ui":{"lead":"People have not only played games, they have painted them. Zoom in on 3,300 years of pictures and figures and look at the details.","details":"Details","links":"See or play the game","record":"About the work","game":"Game","exhibit":"Collection","room":"Room","room_chance":"Chance","hint":"Pinch or scroll to zoom · drag to move · tap a number","r_artist":"Artist","r_date":"Date","r_place":"Place","r_medium":"Medium","r_size":"Size","r_museum":"Museum","r_credit":"Credit line","r_rights":"Image","met":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art","cc0":"Open Access (public domain)","metlink":"The work on the museum’s website","credit":"Images: The Metropolitan Museum of Art, The Cleveland Museum of Art, The Art Institute of Chicago and the Rijksmuseum, Open Access (public domain).","cma":"The Cleveland Museum of Art","aic":"The Art Institute of Chicago","rijks":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam"},"ex":{},"nefertari":{"title":"Queen Nefertari Playing Senet","artist":"Nina de Garis Davies (facsimile)","date":"original ca. 1290–1224 BC · facsimile 1921–22","place":"Egypt (Valley of the Queens)","game":"Senet","medium":"Tempera on paper (facsimile at half size)","size":"43 × 46 cm","credit":"Rogers Fund, 1930","p":["Nefertari was the principal queen of Ramesses II. This is a copy of a painting in her tomb in the Valley of the Queens, made inside the tomb in 1921–22; even the cracks and losses of the wall were copied.","The queen sits before a board and plays senet against an invisible opponent. Painted in a tomb, the game is thought to have meant more than play: winning the journey into the afterlife."],"spots":[["Two kinds of pieces","Pointed pieces and spool-shaped pieces stand on the board: one set for each player."],["The queen’s hand","Her fingers reach for a piece."],["Board and stand","The board is shown from the side, so the squares cannot be seen. It rests on a slender stand."],["Her name","The queen’s name is written inside an oval frame, a cartouche."],["The invisible opponent","No one sits across from her: she plays an unseen opponent."]]},"hydria":{"title":"Achilles and Ajax Playing a Board Game (water jar)","artist":"Attributed to the Leagros Group","date":"ca. 510 BC","place":"Greece (Attica)","game":"A dice game","medium":"Terracotta (black-figure)","size":"H. 54.1 cm","credit":"Fletcher Fund, 1956","p":["During the siege of Troy, two Greek heroes, Achilles and Ajax, pass the time over a board. The scene was invented around 540 BC by the potter and painter Exekias; his original survives on an amphora in the Vatican Museums.","The subject was a great favourite between about 540 and 480 BC, and more than 150 examples are known. On this jar the goddess Athena stands in the middle. The heroes play a game in which pieces move by the roll of dice."],"spots":[["Athena","The goddess Athena stands between the two."],["The hero on the left","Still holding his spear, he bends over the board."],["The hero on the right","He has not put down his weapons either: a moment’s play in the middle of a war."],["The board","The low block between them is the board."],["The shoulder","On the jar’s shoulder a chariot sets off."]]},"liubo":{"title":"Pair of Seated Figures Playing Liubo","artist":"","date":"1st century BC – 1st century AD","place":"China (Han dynasty)","game":"Liubo","medium":"Earthenware with pigment","size":"H. 35.1 cm and 34.6 cm","credit":"Charlotte C. and John C. Weber Collection, Gift of Charlotte C. and John C. Weber, 1992","p":["Two players of liubo, the craze of Han China. Figures like these were placed in tombs, carrying the wish that the pleasures of life would go on after death.","In the photograph the liubo board and bone pieces of this museum’s collection lie between them. The figures catch a dramatic moment in a heated game."],"spots":[["The liubo board","The same board as the collection’s “Liubo Board and Pieces”: see the T, L and V marks."],["The player on the left","He studies the board calmly."],["The player on the right","Hand raised, mouth wide open: has he just made a winning move?"],["Pieces on the board","Bone pieces stand at both ends of the board."],["Pieces off the board","More pieces lie on the floor."]]},"shahnama":{"title":"“Buzurjmihr Masters the Hindu Game of Chess”, from the Shahnama of Shah Tahmasp","artist":"Painting attributed to ʿAbd al-Vahhab","date":"ca. 1530–35","place":"Iran (Tabriz)","game":"Chess","medium":"Opaque watercolor, ink, silver and gold on paper","size":"Painting 24.4 × 17.5 cm (page 47.3 × 31.8 cm)","credit":"Gift of Arthur A. Houghton Jr., 1970","p":["A scene from Firdausi’s epic, the Shahnama (Book of Kings). The king of India sends an envoy with a chessboard and pieces and challenges the Persian king Nushirvan: work out how the game is played, and India will pay tribute.","Only the young sage Buzurjmihr solves it in time. In the Shahnama, Persia then sends backgammon in return, and the Indians cannot solve it. King and courtiers stare at the board and the players."],"spots":[["The chessboard","Pieces stand on a squared board, so small that their shapes are hard to make out."],["King Nushirvan","On his throne, he waits to see the riddle solved."],["Round the board","The players lean over the board."],["The verses","The panels above and below carry Firdausi’s verses."],["The courtiers","Men in turbans with tall red batons stand and watch."]]},"chaupar":{"title":"Shiva and Parvati Playing Chaupar, from a Rasamanjari series","artist":"Devidasa of Nurpur","date":"1694–95","place":"India (Basohli, Jammu)","game":"Chaupar (a relative of Ludo’s ancestor)","medium":"Opaque watercolor, ink, silver and gold on paper","size":"Image 16.5 × 27.6 cm","credit":"Gift of Dr. J. C. Burnett, 1957","p":["A page from a series illustrating the Rasamanjari, a 15th-century Sanskrit love poem. The god Shiva and his wife Parvati sit on a tiger skin around a cross-shaped board.","Shiva has just cheated Parvati out of a necklace, and she pleads with him. Bold colour, an uncertain depth of space, and colour and gesture used for their meaning: these are the marks of the Basohli school."],"spots":[["The cross-shaped board","Chaupar and pachisi are played on a board shaped like this cross."],["White bars on the board","Probably the long dice (pasa) thrown in chaupar."],["Shiva","White-bodied, with a snake round his neck."],["Parvati","Her hand held out, she asks for her necklace back."],["The tiger skin","The two sit on a tiger skin."]]},"shogi":{"title":"A Young Woman and Man Playing Shōgi (“Chūnagon Kanesuke”, from the Thirty-Six Poetic Immortals)","artist":"Suzuki Harunobu","date":"ca. 1767–69","place":"Japan (Edo)","game":"Shogi","medium":"Woodblock print; ink and color on paper","size":"28.3 × 20.6 cm","credit":"Henry L. Phillips Collection, Bequest of Henry L. Phillips, 1939","p":["A print from a series that alludes to the Thirty-Six Poetic Immortals; this one stands for the poet Chūnagon Kanesuke. On a summer night, beside a mosquito net, a young woman and man sit over a shogi board.","Harunobu was the artist who made full-colour prints, printed from many blocks, popular. Even the pieces on the board are drawn in detail."],"spots":[["A shogi board on feet","A thick board on legs, just like today’s."],["A small box","Probably the box for the pieces."],["The mosquito net","A green net hangs over the room: it is a summer night."],["The lantern","A standing lamp lights the board."],["The poem","The cloud-shaped panel carries the poet’s name and a poem."]]},"sugoroku":{"title":"Two Young Women Playing a Game of Sugoroku","artist":"Utagawa Toyokuni I","date":"Edo period (late 18th – early 19th century)","place":"Japan (Edo)","game":"Ban-sugoroku (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Diptych of woodblock prints","size":"Each 38.1 × 25.6 cm","credit":"H. O. Havemeyer Collection, Gift of Mrs. J. Watson Webb, 1930","p":["Two prints joined into one wide picture: young women gathered in a room enjoy a game of ban-sugoroku. The game is a relative of backgammon, of the same line as the Shōsōin’s sugoroku board in this collection.","White and black stones stand on the board, and the young woman on the right holds a cup for shaking the dice. Mount Fuji shows through the window."],"spots":[["The board","White and black stones stand on its long points."],["The dice cup","The dice are shaken in this cup."],["Stones off the board","Stones already borne off, or not yet entered?"],["Mount Fuji","Fuji beyond the window."],["The join","Here the two prints meet."]]},"eakins":{"title":"The Chess Players","artist":"Thomas Eakins","date":"1876","place":"United States (Philadelphia)","game":"Chess","medium":"Oil on wood","size":"29.8 × 42.6 cm","credit":"Gift of the artist, 1881","p":["In the parlour of a Philadelphia home, two friends play chess while the artist’s father, Benjamin Eakins, looks on. Eakins learned his careful construction of space and his precise detail from his teacher in Paris, Gérôme.","In 1881 The Chess Players became the first work the Metropolitan Museum accepted as a gift from a living artist."],"spots":[["The chessmen","In the dim room, the pieces on the board stand out in the light."],["The artist’s father","Standing and watching the game: Benjamin Eakins."],["The player on the right","The painter George W. Holmes, a friend of the artist’s father."],["The drawer","A Latin inscription: “Benjamin Eakins’s son painted this in ’76.”"],["Over the mantel","A reproduction of a painting by Eakins’s teacher Gérôme."],["The cat","On the floor, only the cat seems uninterested in the game."]]},"weiqi":{"title":"Dish with immortals playing weiqi","artist":"Unknown","date":"1425–36 (Ming dynasty, Xuande period)","place":"China","game":"Go (weiqi)","medium":"Carved red and green lacquer","size":"H. 3.5, W. 14.9, L. 23.5 cm","credit":"Gift of Florence and Herbert Irving, 2015","p":["A dish of carved lacquer: dozens of coats were built up and then carved, with a green layer under the red so that the depth of the cut brings out the colours. Inside the lobed rim, immortals play go beneath a pine.","China has the legend of Lanke, the “rotten axe handle”: the woodcutter Wang Zhi watched immortals playing go in the mountains, and found that so much time had passed that his axe handle had rotted. Immortals at the board were a favourite subject. Who the standing figure is, the records do not say."],"spots":[["The board","The go board between the two immortals, carved down to its lines."],["The immortals at play","Two figures seated by the rocks around the board."],["The standing figure","A figure standing a little apart. He recalls the woodcutter of the Lanke legend, but who he is is not known."],["The moon","A round moon in the sky (or perhaps the sun)."],["The pine","A spreading pine, a tree often standing for long life and the land of the immortals."],["The peony rim","Large peonies and leaves are carved round the rim."]]},"webb":{"title":"The Backgammon Players (cabinet)","artist":"Philip Webb (designer), Edward Burne-Jones (painter), Morris, Marshall, Faulkner & Co. (maker)","date":"1861","place":"Britain (London)","game":"Backgammon","medium":"Painted pine, oil paint on leather, brass, copper","size":"H. 185.4, W. 114.3, D. 53.3 cm","credit":"Rogers Fund, 1926","p":["One of the earliest pieces of furniture from the firm William Morris founded with friends in 1861. On the leather of the doors, a man and a woman in medieval dress sit over a game of backgammon, painted in oils. It was shown at the London International Exhibition of 1862.","It sums up the Arts and Crafts aim of erasing the line between the fine and the applied arts. The background of the leather is punched with tiny dots, a medieval technique."],"spots":[["The board","The backgammon board on the table between them."],["The man","A young man, a piece in hand, studies the board."],["The woman","Facing him, a woman holding a piece (or a dice cup)."],["Flowers at their feet","Flowers bloom at the players’ feet."],["Punched leather","The leather background is punched with fine dots that catch the light."],["The lozenge bands","Bands of lozenges on gold frame the doors above and below."]]},"liberale":{"title":"The Chess Players","artist":"Liberale da Verona","date":"ca. 1475","place":"Italy (Verona)","game":"Chess","medium":"Tempera on wood","size":"34.9 × 41.3 cm","credit":"Maitland F. Griggs Collection, Bequest of Maitland F. Griggs, 1943","p":["A fragment from the front of a cassone, the decorated chest that held a Renaissance Italian bride’s belongings. Liberale was also known for illuminating the choir books of Siena Cathedral.","It shows a moment from an unidentified love story. On the companion fragment a maiden at a window seems to beckon a youth inside; here the two play chess. According to the museum, she is about to lose. Chess was a favourite figure of courtship in the love literature of the time."],"spots":[["The chessboard","A red and white board set with dark pieces; the game seems to be near its end."],["The youth","He reaches toward the board — and looks set to win."],["The maiden","Seated opposite; on the companion panel she beckons him from a window."],["The window","Beyond the open window, a hill and the sky."],["Young men watching","Men with folded arms follow the game."],["Young women watching","Women peer at the game from behind the column."]]},"mirror":{"title":"Mirror case with a couple playing chess","artist":"Unknown","date":"1325–50","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Chess","medium":"Ivory","size":"Diameter 10.2 cm","credit":"Purchase from the J. H. Wade Fund, 1940","p":["This palm-sized ivory disc once covered a hand mirror. In fourteenth-century Paris a polished metal mirror was held between two ivory plaques and carried about; carved ivories like this were luxuries prized by courtly and aristocratic owners.","Inside a curtained tent, a young man and a woman play chess. In medieval romance, chess stood for both love and war. Some have suggested the couple are Tristan and Iseult, or Lancelot and Guinevere, but no one knows. The museum asks its visitors: who do you think is winning?"],"spots":[["The chessboard","A board on a one-legged table, with a few pieces standing on it."],["The young man","He reaches across to make his next move."],["The lady","A woman in a hood; her fingertips touch a piece on the board."],["The closed hand","Her other hand is closed in her lap. Is she holding a piece she has taken?"],["The tent","Curtains tied back on both sides — a favourite setting for chess on mirror cases."]]},"morris":{"title":"A gaming board (Nine Men’s Morris)","artist":"Peter Flötner","date":"1500–46","place":"Germany","game":"Nine Men’s Morris","medium":"Woodcut","credit":"Charles W. Harkness Endowment Fund, 1923","p":["A woodcut by Peter Flötner, one of the most gifted sculptors of Renaissance Nuremberg, who also designed ornament, furniture, goldsmiths’ models and a famously satirical pack of playing cards. Hidden beneath the swirling foliage is the board of Nine Men’s Morris — “Mühle” in German: three nested squares joined at the middle of each side.","The board has 24 points. Line up three of your pieces (a mill) and you take one of your opponent’s. Prints like this also served craftsmen as patterns, and the museum showed this sheet in an exhibition of goldsmiths’ models and designs. Whether people played on the paper itself, or copied it in wood or metal, is not recorded."],"spots":[["The central square","The innermost square, filled with fine interlace."],["An outer corner","A corner of the outer square — one of the 24 points where pieces stand."],["A middle corner","The corners of the middle square are points too."],["The joining lines","Bands join the three squares at the middle of each side; pieces move along them as well."],["A mask","Scrolling cartouches with faces at the top, bottom and sides — Renaissance grotesque ornament."],["The vine border","Leaves and fruit cut out white against the black ground."]]},"leifeng":{"title":"Leifeng Pagoda board game","artist":"Unknown","date":"1736–95 (Qianlong period)","place":"China","game":"Race game","medium":"Woodblock print; ink on paper","size":"31 × 29.8 cm","credit":"Purchase from the J. H. Wade Fund, 2025","p":["A single printed sheet made to be played on, from the reign of the Qianlong emperor of Qing China. Its squares are filled with the cast of the Legend of the White Snake: the snake spirit Bai Suzhen (“White Lady”), her companion Xiaoqing (“Little Green”), the young man Xu Xuan (Xu Xian in later versions), and the monk Fahai, who at the end imprisons the White Lady beneath Leifeng Pagoda by West Lake.","The track spirals in from a square marked 起馬, “set off”, to the pagoda at the centre. Players probably raced through the story with dice, much as in the Western Game of the Goose; the rules are not recorded. According to the museum, colour prints like this were sold in the cities and hung on walls, part of urban visual culture — each colour printed from its own carved block."],"spots":[["Start","The square marked 起馬, “set off” — probably the starting point."],["White Lady and Little Green","The snake spirit Bai Suzhen and her companion Xiaoqing."],["Xu Xuan","The young man who marries the White Lady."],["The monk Fahai","The monk who imprisons her beneath the pagoda."],["Leifeng Pagoda","The pagoda by West Lake in Hangzhou, where the story ends."],["The centre","A woman seated before the pagoda and a kneeling man — the story’s last scene, and perhaps the goal."]]},"tutinama":{"title":"The wounded monkey bites the hand of the prince, his chessmate (from a Tuti-nama, Tales of a Parrot)","artist":"Dasavanta","date":"c. 1560","place":"India (Mughal court of Akbar)","game":"Chess","medium":"Gum tempera, ink and gold on paper","size":"20 × 14.2 cm (painting 10.2 × 10.6 cm)","credit":"Gift of Mrs. A. Dean Perry, 1962","p":["An illustration from the Tuti-nama, the “Tales of a Parrot”, made at the court of the Mughal emperor Akbar. It is one of the stories a mother parrot tells her chicks to warn them against friendships with other kinds of creatures, such as the fox cubs. In it, a monkey who was good at chess often played with a prince.","One day, with guests present, the monkey made an unkind joke and the prince slapped him — and the monkey bit the prince’s hand. Outside on the right, servants prepare food while the astonished guests look on. An inscription at the bottom names Dasavanta, who later became one of the most celebrated masters at the Mughal court; as a young artist he laid a cool blue layer under his painting."],"spots":[["The monkey","Biting the prince’s hand — his partner at chess."],["The prince","The prince whose hand is bitten."],["The chessboard","A red-squared board on the floor."],["The astonished guests","The guests invited to the gathering."],["Preparing food","Servants outside, setting pots on the fire."],["The Persian text","Above, the story is written out in Persian."]]},"teniers":{"title":"Game of Backgammon","artist":"David Teniers","date":"1640s","place":"Flanders","game":"Backgammon","medium":"Oil on wood, transferred to canvas","size":"59 × 80.6 cm","credit":"Gift of Mr. and Mrs. Samuel D. Wise, 1943","p":["A high-stakes game of backgammon by the Flemish painter Teniers. Backgammon is very old; its modern form appeared in the 1400s, and the name “backgammon” was coined at about the time this picture was painted. In the 1600s the game was hugely popular and often played for money.","Onlookers crowd around the two players, and against the wall someone keeps score. From the women watching — one leans from a window — the museum takes the room to be a gaming room in a brothel. Scenes of play like this often carried a moral: gambling was foolish, even wicked, and a waste of time."],"spots":[["The board","An open backgammon board on the table, the game under way."],["The man in white","A player seen from behind, reaching for his men."],["The man with his chin in his hand","The opponent, deep in thought."],["Keeping score","A man marking a board on the wall — the score, or the stakes?"],["The woman at the window","An older woman watching from above."],["The back room","Men drink beside a young woman in the room beyond."]]},"imari":{"title":"Dish with two men playing go","artist":"Unknown","date":"1700s","place":"Japan (Edo period)","game":"Go","medium":"Porcelain, molded, with overglaze enamel (Hizen ware, Imari style)","size":"2.4 × 13.8 cm","credit":"Severance and Greta Millikin Collection, 1964","p":["On this small square dish, two men play go under a pine tree: one in yellow sits facing the board, set straight on the ground, while his opponent in green leans back against the tree.","It is Imari porcelain from Hizen (today’s Saga and Nagasaki), and its greens recall Kutani enamels. It was made for export, after Japan had replaced China as Europe’s main supplier of porcelain."],"spots":[["The go board","A board ruled in squares, set straight on the ground."],["The man in yellow","Seated, facing the board."],["The man in green","His opponent, leaning back against the tree and smiling."],["The pine","A pine stretches its branches overhead."],["The red frame","A red frame with cut corners surrounds the picture."]]},"shoei":{"title":"The Four Accomplishments (one of a pair of screens)","artist":"attributed to Kano Shōei","date":"late 1500s–early 1600s","place":"Japan (Momoyama period)","game":"Go","medium":"One of a pair of six-panel folding screens; ink and slight color on paper","size":"153 × 358.6 cm","credit":"John L. Severance Fund, 1979","p":["A six-panel screen attributed to Kano Shōei of the Kano school. With its partner it shows the “Four Accomplishments” expected of a cultured Chinese gentleman — playing the qin, playing qi (go), calligraphy and painting. Here elite Chinese gentlemen and their servants relax in the countryside beneath the pines.","At the centre two men play go while a third watches. Nearby a table holds books and a qin, still in its cloth case, waiting to be played. The scene balances enjoyment and formality. The museum points out that one of the servants is distracted by the movement of a fish in the water."],"spots":[["The go board","A footed board; two gentlemen play across it."],["The gentlemen","Two players and a third who watches."],["The table","Probably the table with books and the qin still in its case."],["The servants","Attendants waiting by the table."],["The distracted servant","Lying by the water, watching a fish move."],["The pine","A great pine spreads its branches over the scene."]]},"umaya":{"title":"Horse Stable (go and shogi on the veranda)","artist":"Unknown","date":"Early 1500s","place":"Japan (Muromachi period)","game":"Go and shogi","medium":"One of a pair of six-panel folding screens; ink, color and gold on paper","size":"146.1 × 346.6 cm","credit":"Edward L. Whittemore Fund, 1934","p":["This folding screen of six joined panels is one of a pair. Prized horses in their stalls may be the main theme, but a lot is happening on the veranda in front.","There, people challenge one another at two different board games: go on the left and shogi on the right. As the museum puts it, the lively mood comes from the interplay of the restless horses and the quiet strategy of the games. In Japan monkeys were thought to protect horses, and were sometimes kept in stables."],"spots":[["The go board","A finely ruled board with black stones on it."],["Two men in black caps","Men in tall black eboshi caps: the one in green reaches for the board, the one in red looks on."],["The shogi board","A board with large squares — far fewer than on the go board."],["The laughing opponent","A shaven-headed monk picks up a piece while the man opposite clutches his head and roars with laughter. Has someone just scored a point?"],["A monkey","At the end of the veranda — a monkey in a red coat?"],["A horse","A black horse rears up, held by its ropes."]]},"goryeo":{"title":"Pitcher with inlaid figures playing go","artist":"Unknown","date":"1200s","place":"Korea (Goryeo dynasty)","game":"Go (baduk)","medium":"Celadon ware with inlaid white and black slip","size":"H. 20.4 cm, diam. 14.3 cm","credit":"Gift of John L. Severance, 1917","p":["A Goryeo celadon pitcher, with a spout and a handle. Inlay — carving lines into the clay, filling them with white and black slip, then glazing and firing — was the specialty of Goryeo potters, and here two figures sitting under a willow stand out in white inside a round frame.","Between them is a board, and the museum compares the scene to pictures of playing go — baduk in Korean. Playing the zither, go, calligraphy and painting were the “four accomplishments” of the cultivated scholar, and their elegant gatherings became designs for craftsmen too. Korean inlaid celadon showing people is rare."],"spots":[["The board","The board between the two, shown as a white square."],["The figure on the left","Seated, facing the board."],["The figure on the right","The opponent, sitting across from him."],["The willow","Willow branches hang over them, drawn in black slip."],["The cloud frame","Swirling cloud-like scrolls run around the round frame."],["The crackle","Fine cracks in the glaze. They form as the pot cools, because clay and glaze shrink by different amounts."]]},"kangra":{"title":"Couple playing chaupar on a terrace","artist":"Unknown","date":"c. 1790–1800","place":"India (Pahari, Kangra school)","game":"Chaupar","medium":"Drawing with red ochre underdrawing","size":"19.4 × 13.2 cm","credit":"Edward L. Whittemore Fund, 1936","p":["A drawing by an artist of Kangra, in the Pahari hills at the foot of the Himalayas. The first sketch was made in red (red ochre) and then refined in black. With no color laid over it, every line of the figures and their things can be followed.","A man and a woman sit on a terrace on either side of a cross-shaped board, playing chaupar — an old Indian race game on the same cross as pachisi, moved by throwing long dice (pasa). The squares have not yet been drawn on the board. Attendants wait on the pair."],"spots":[["The cross-shaped board","A board in the shape of a cross, on the floor. Its squares are not drawn yet."],["The woman","Her palm turned up — about to throw the dice?"],["The man","A turban with a plume; he reaches toward the board."],["The attendant on the left","She holds a small flask and stretches out a cloth."],["The attendant on the right","She holds a long-handled implement and a small vessel."],["The red underdrawing","Around the turban, the red first sketch shows beneath the black lines."]]},"vuillard":{"title":"The Game of Checkers (from Landscapes and Interiors)","artist":"Édouard Vuillard","date":"1899","place":"France","game":"Checkers","medium":"Color lithograph (state III/III); printed by Auguste Clot, published by Ambroise Vollard","size":"Image 33 × 25.7 cm (sheet 38 × 30.8 cm)","credit":"Gift of the Hanna Fund, 1951","p":["Ambroise Vollard, a dealer in Impressionist paintings, was also an important publisher of prints. In 1895 he commissioned a portfolio of 12 color lithographs from Pierre Bonnard, and many more albums followed — among them Vuillard’s Landscapes and Interiors, printed by the master printer Auguste Clot.","Bonnard and Vuillard belonged to the Nabis — Hebrew for “prophets” — who sought a new art in which form and color themselves carried feeling, and who loved scenes of city life and middle-class home life. In a room crowded with pattern, two men face each other over a checkers board while a woman stands and watches. In French, checkers is “dames”; the pencil note at lower left reads “partie de dames”, a game of checkers."],"spots":[["The board","A small checkered board, with the pieces set out on it."],["The man on the left","A bearded man reaches out over the board."],["The man on the right","His opponent, bent over the board in an armchair, half hidden by the standing woman."],["The standing woman","Hand to her chin, she follows the game."],["The lamp","A lamp by the wall. Wallpaper and upholstery alike are full of pattern."],["The pencil note","At lower left, in pencil: “partie de dames” — a game of checkers."]]},"talbot":{"title":"The Chess Players (early photograph)","artist":"Attributed to William Henry Fox Talbot","date":"c. 1843–47","place":"England","game":"Chess","medium":"Salted paper print","size":"19.9 × 15 cm","credit":"Purchased with funds provided by Mr. and Mrs. Everett Kovler","p":["The negative-positive process invented by William Henry Fox Talbot, with the endless copies it made possible, dominated photography until the digital age. His early experiments show both his interest in science and his artistic aspirations, and scholars have confirmed that he took ten or more views of chess players.","Yet this one may not be his. This print and other versions of the same scene are unsigned and on a different paper from Talbot’s usual stock. The sitters are probably the photographer Antoine Claudet and Talbot’s assistant Nicolaas Henneman; recent research suggests the pictures were made in Claudet’s studio, passed to Henneman, and reached Talbot when Henneman gave him prints toward a debt (the museum’s note)."],"spots":[["The board","The chessboard on the table, its pieces lined up."],["The man in the top hat","Hat still on, he reaches for a piece."],["The thinker","Hand at his chin, his opponent studies the board."],["The curtain","A curtain hangs behind — a backdrop for the picture?"],["Another hat","A second top hat sits on the stand at right."]]},"pvl":{"title":"Men Playing a Game of Dice","artist":"Master PVL","date":"c. 1520","place":"Netherlands","game":"Dice","medium":"Engraving on paper","size":"9.4 × 9.4 cm (round)","credit":"Charles Hack and the Hearn Family Trust Collection, purchased 2023","p":["The work of an unnamed engraver active in the Netherlands in the early 1500s, known as Master PVL from the monogram “PVL” cut at lower left. On a round print small enough to sit in the palm of a hand, dense fine lines build the depth of the room and the feel of the clothes.","Two dice lie on the table. A man leaning in and a youth with a fur collar point and argue — over the count, perhaps. Women look on from behind, and a jug of wine stands under the table. Dice have been tied to gambling since antiquity, and were banned again and again."],"spots":[["The dice","Two dice tumbling on the table."],["Pointing hands","Two fingers meet on the table — an argument over the count?"],["The man in the broad hat","Leaning in. His slashed, puffed sleeves were the fashion of the day."],["The youth with the fur collar","A youth in a great hat and a fur collar."],["The women watching","Women peer at the game from behind."],["The monogram","“PVL” at lower left — the only clue to the artist’s name."]]},"kiyohiro":{"title":"Sugoroku Players","artist":"Torii Kiyohiro","date":"c. 1750","place":"Japan (Edo period)","game":"Ban-sugoroku","medium":"Color woodblock print (benizuri-e), ōban; published by Urokogataya","size":"43.5 × 29.3 cm","credit":"Clarence Buckingham Collection","p":["Torii Kiyohiro was a ukiyo-e artist active from about 1737 to 1776. This is a benizuri-e, printed from a few color blocks — pink beni, green and the like — in the years before full-color nishiki-e prints appeared in 1765.","The game on the black stand is ban-sugoroku: two players throw dice and move white and black pieces, and the first to bear all their pieces off wins. It belongs to the same family as backgammon, and ukiyo-e prints often show women enjoying it. Behind them a woman smokes a long pipe and watches."],"spots":[["The board","A sugoroku board on its stand, with white and black pieces."],["The player on the left","Reaching out to move a piece."],["The woman on the right","Bent over the board, the opponent studies the pieces."],["The woman with the pipe","With a long kiseru pipe, she follows the game from behind."],["The raised hand","The woman on the right holds up something small and black — the dice cup?"],["The signature","Signed “Torii Kiyohiro hitsu”, with the seal of the publisher Urokogataya."]]},"ingram":{"title":"The Game of Chinese Chess","artist":"John Ingram, after François Boucher","date":"1741–63","place":"France (Paris)","game":"Xiangqi","medium":"Etching and engraving","size":"Sheet 39.4 × 27.7 cm (plate 24.5 × 16.4 cm)","credit":"The Elisha Whittelsey Collection, 1966","p":["A drawing by the French painter François Boucher, engraved by the London-born printmaker John Ingram. Ingram moved to Paris and, between 1741 and 1763, made a series of prints after Boucher’s chinoiserie — China as Europeans imagined it. The title below reads “LE JEU D'ECHETS CHINOIS”, the game of Chinese chess.","A woman with her hair piled high moves a piece on the board in her lap while a small child looks on. The game is taken to be xiangqi, yet real xiangqi pieces are all flat discs, while here tall pieces stand beside little square ones. The palm tree and the figure seated on the urn, too, belong less to China than to the faraway land that Europe dreamed of at the time."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The board on her lap, with tall pieces and square ones. Real xiangqi pieces are flat discs."],["The hand at a piece","Her right hand moves a piece."],["Chin on hand","Her left hand at her chin, she looks down at the board."],["The watching child","A child stands by the board, following the game."],["The figure on the urn","A figure sits cross-legged on top of a great urn."],["Title and names","“Dessiné par Fr. Boucher”, “Gravé par Ingram” and the title “LE JEU D'ECHETS CHINOIS”."]]},"posada":{"title":"Game of the Goose","artist":"José Guadalupe Posada","date":"1909","place":"Mexico","game":"Game of the Goose","medium":"Relief engraving on zinc on paper","size":"29.3 × 39.4 cm","credit":"William McCallin McKee Memorial Endowment","p":["A single sheet to play on, made by the Mexican printmaker Posada — famous for his skeleton calaveras — for the Mexico City publisher Antonio Vanegas Arroyo. The banner at lower left reads “JUEGO DE LA OCA” (game of the goose), and the track spirals from square 1 to 63 at the centre.","The rules (EXPLICACION) are printed in the middle: a first throw of 5 and 4 goes to 26, of 6 and 3 to 53. Land on a goose (a bird) and you count the same again; at the inn (Venta, 19) you wait two turns; in the well (Poso, 31) and the prison (Carcel, 52) you stay until someone else comes; the labyrinth (42) sends you back to 39, and death (Muerte, 58) back to the start. You must land exactly on 63. Players also paid stakes when they landed on certain squares — it was a betting game too."],"spots":[["The rules","The “EXPLICACION” in the middle: what each square does, and when to pay a stake."],["Square 1","The start. From here the track spirals in toward the centre."],["The well (31)","A well like a fountain. Fall in and you stay until someone else comes."],["Death (58)","The skull square. Land here and you go back to the start."],["The title banner","“JUEGO DE LA OCA” on the banner at lower left, with children at play beneath it."],["The publisher’s name","The goddess’s banner reads “IMPRENTA DE A. VANEGAS ARROYO”, the house that published many of Posada’s prints."]]},"jintile":{"title":"Tile with Women Playing a Board Game","artist":"Unknown","date":"1200s","place":"China (Jin dynasty)","game":"A board game (probably go)","medium":"Earthenware (molded relief)","size":"H. 27.3 cm, W. 24.8 cm, D. 5.4 cm","credit":"Purchase, Friends of Asian Art Gifts, 2002","p":["A relief tile made under the Jin (1115–1234), the dynasty of the Jurchen people who ruled north China. Under a flowering tree, two women sit facing each other on a platform with a board between them.","The board is cut with a fine grid, so they are probably playing go. The woman on the right leans in and points at the board while the one on the left watches. Many Jin-period tombs in north China were lined with bricks carved in relief with scenes of daily life, and this tile probably decorated such a tomb."],"spots":[["The board","A finely ruled board — probably a go board."],["The pointing hand","The woman on the right points at the board. Her next move?"],["The woman on the left","Hair piled high, she watches the board."],["The woman on the right","Leaning in over the board."],["The flowering tree","Branches of a flowering tree spread over them."],["The platform","The platform they sit on, with a scalloped apron between its legs."]]},"kyosai":{"title":"Farting Upsets a Game of Go (from a sketch album)","artist":"Kawanabe Kyōsai","date":"Mid- to late 1800s","place":"Japan (Edo to Meiji period)","game":"Go","medium":"One opening of an accordion-style album of 26 double leaves; ink and color on paper","size":"Album (closed) 28 × 18 cm","credit":"Given by Anne and Anthony d’Offay in honor of Evan Turner","p":["A scene from a sketch album by Kawanabe Kyōsai (1831–1889), a painter active from the last years of the shoguns into the Meiji era, drawn in quick, free brushstrokes on the pages of a folding album. Trained in the Kanō school’s exacting technique, Kyōsai was also loved for his demons, caricatures and satire.","The museum’s title says it all: “Farting Upsets a Game of Go.” On the left a man in a tall black cap bends over; on the right the go board flies through the air, the stone bowls rain stones, and his opponent is knocked flat on his back. A serious game ruined in a single blast — Kyōsai sets the old Japanese tradition of fart humor, already found in medieval scrolls, down with his rapid brush."],"spots":[["The man in the black cap","Bent over, his backside thrust out — the cause of the trouble?"],["The flying board","The ruled go board sails off at an angle."],["The bowls and stones","Two stone bowls tumble, and the stones pour down."],["The fan","An open fan is blown away beside the board."],["The toppled opponent","Flat on his back, eyes wide."],["The brushwork","Strong ink lines, as if drawn in one go without a sketch."]]},"yokuts":{"title":"Gambling Tray","artist":"Yokuts weaver (name unknown)","date":"c. 1900","place":"United States (near Dunlap, California)","game":"Huuchuish (walnut-shell dice)","medium":"Plant fibers (coiled)","size":"H. 5.1 cm, diam. 61 cm","credit":"Purchased with funds provided by Mrs. Leonard S. Florsheim, Jr.","p":["Many of California’s Indigenous peoples played games of chance. This tray was used for huuchuish, a dice game played only by women: eight walnut-shell dice were thrown onto the tray, and sticks were used to keep score.","The circular design woven into the tray shows the game itself — the dice, the score sticks and the players (the museum’s note). It is a piece of equipment that carries a picture of its own game. In the photograph, walnut-shell dice lie on and around the tray."],"spots":[["The players","Figures circle the rim — the people who join the game."],["Score sticks","Bundles of short upright lines: the sticks used to keep score."],["Round motifs","Small round motifs — the dice, perhaps?"],["A walnut die","A walnut-shell die. Eight were thrown onto the tray."],["The band of triangles","A band of dark triangles runs all the way round."],["The centre","The coil spirals out from the middle, ringed by a dashed circle."]]},"hokkei":{"title":"Ushiwakamaru Defeats Benkei at Sugoroku","artist":"Totoya Hokkei","date":"c. 1825","place":"Japan (Edo period)","game":"Ban-sugoroku","medium":"Surimono (color woodblock print, shikishiban)","size":"21.8 × 19.1 cm","credit":"Gift of Helen C. Gunsaulus","p":["A surimono by Totoya Hokkei (1780–1850), a pupil of Hokusai. Surimono were prints privately commissioned — often by poetry circles, with their own verses — and finished more lavishly than commercial ukiyo-e, with fine paper and metallic pigments.","Ushiwakamaru, the boyhood name of the hero Minamoto no Yoshitsune, and the giant warrior-monk Benkei. Their famous meeting was a duel on Kyoto’s Gojō Bridge, but here the contest becomes a game of sugoroku: the boy sits calmly beyond the board while Benkei, his halberd on his back, scratches his head in defeat — a playful re-staging, it seems."],"spots":[["The sugoroku board","A box-shaped board with white and black pieces."],["Benkei scratching his head","In full armour, the beaten Benkei scratches his head."],["Ushiwakamaru","The boy hero, seated beyond the board."],["The halberd","The blade of Benkei’s great naginata."],["The poem","Verses at upper right — probably by the circle that commissioned the print."],["The signature","Hokkei’s signature in the cartouche at lower right."]]},"vorsterman":{"title":"Backgammon Players (a night scene)","artist":"Lucas Emil Vorsterman, after Adam de Coster","date":"c. 1630","place":"Flanders (Antwerp)","game":"Backgammon","medium":"Engraving on ivory paper","size":"Image 25.5 × 34.4 cm","credit":"Bernard F. Rogers Collection","p":["Adam de Coster was a leading painter of candlelit night scenes in Antwerp around 1630, when Vorsterman, in the same city, was engraving works after popular contemporaries. A pupil of Rubens, Vorsterman fell out with his master and went to England, after which he made stronger, more stylized, Caravaggesque engravings like this one.","Around a table in a dark room, men gather over a backgammon board. Following De Coster’s handling of light, two candle flames light up the faces around the table (the museum’s note). On the right a young lute player looks out at us."],"spots":[["The board","A backgammon board opened like a box on the table."],["The candle","A candle in its holder; its flame lights the faces."],["The man with the pipe","In a broad hat, he smokes and watches the game."],["The man from behind","Leaning over, hands on the table."],["The lute player","A youth with a lute, smiling out at us."],["The names","“A. De Coster pi.” at lower left, “Vorsterman” at lower right."]]},"zorn":{"title":"Chess-Players","artist":"Anders Zorn","date":"1891","place":"Sweden","game":"Chess","medium":"Etching on ivory laid paper","size":"Image 13.5 × 9.6 cm (plate 13.9 × 10 cm)","credit":"Wallace L. DeWolf and Charles Deering Collections of Etchings by Anders Zorn","p":["An etching by the Swedish artist Anders Zorn (1860–1920), who won international fame for his portraits in oil and watercolour and also produced a large body of etchings. The plate is no bigger than a hand.","Fast, slanting lines laid one over another are all it takes to bring a dark room and two people out of the copper. The man in front, his back to us, leans over the board; the figure opposite looks down, thinking about the next move. The pieces are drawn on the board in thin strokes. Who sat for it is not recorded."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The light area below is the board; the short upright strokes are pieces."],["The man in front","His back to us, he bends over the board."],["The opponent","Head bowed, eyes on the board."],["The dark background","The depths of the room, built of upright lines."],["Slanting lines","The coat is made of quick diagonal strokes laid over each other."],["The signature","Signed “Zorn” in pencil below the plate."]]},"harunobu-sugoroku":{"title":"Watching the Game (sugoroku)","artist":"Suzuki Harunobu","date":"c. 1766","place":"Japan (Edo period)","game":"Ban-sugoroku","medium":"Color woodblock print (chūban, surimono)","size":"28.5 × 21 cm","credit":"Clarence Buckingham Collection","p":["Suzuki Harunobu (1725?–1770) was the central figure when full-color nishiki-e prints, printed from many blocks, appeared around 1765. This is a privately issued surimono, and the soft, layered colors show what was new.","In a room with plum blossoms, two young women play at a box-shaped sugoroku board while a third stands and looks down at it. White and black pieces line the board; the girl on the left moves a piece and the one on the right reaches for the board. The sliding door behind is painted with a waterfall and pines."],"spots":[["The sugoroku board","A box-shaped ban-sugoroku board with white and black pieces."],["The girl on the left","In a kimono patterned with paper cranes, moving a piece."],["The girl on the right","Bending forward, reaching for the board."],["The onlooker","Standing, she peers at the game, holding a long-handled object."],["The plum","A white plum stretches its branches from above."],["The painted door","A sliding door painted with a waterfall and pines."]]},"kota":{"title":"Maharao Kishor Singh II of Kota Worshiping Krishna as Brij Rajji","artist":"Unknown","date":"c. 1825","place":"India (Rajasthan, Kota)","game":"Pachisi","medium":"Gum tempera, silver and gold on paper","size":"24.5 × 18.8 cm","credit":"James Albert and Mary Gardiner Ford Memorial Fund","p":["The ruler of the small princely state of Kota (r. 1819–28), dressed as a priest of the Pushti Marg sect, offers a lamp with five flames. In the little shrine on the altar stand metal images of Krishna flanked by two consorts — an icon worshipped only in Kota.","Many glass lamps hang from the ceiling, and on the steps up to the altar are toys and games to amuse Krishna and his companions: brass elephants, silver cows, balls and a pachisi board (the museum’s note). The red cross-shaped cloth is that board — pachisi set out as a plaything for the god."],"spots":[["The pachisi board","The red cross-shaped cloth on the steps: a pachisi board."],["Elephants","Two brass elephants stand beside the board."],["Balls","Golden balls on the step above."],["Krishna","The images of Krishna and two consorts in the little shrine."],["The ruler and his lamp","Dressed as a priest, the ruler offers a lamp with five flames."],["Glass lamps","A host of glass lamps hangs from the ceiling."]]},"coyote":{"title":"Game of the Coyote","artist":"Follower of José Guadalupe Posada","date":"Undated","place":"Mexico","game":"Coyote and hens (a fox-and-geese game)","medium":"Relief engraving on zinc on paper","size":"38.2 × 30.5 cm","credit":"William McCallin McKee Memorial Endowment","p":["A single sheet to play on, printed from zinc relief like the sheets Posada made for Mexico City’s popular presses. Across the top runs “JUEGO DEL COYOTE” (game of the coyote); around the board, coyotes raid the henhouse and a man swings a stick to drive one off. The strip below reads “Property of Ildefonso T. Orellana.”","The rules are printed in the “EXPLICACION” at the bottom: two players, one with fifteen hens and the other with a single coyote. Hens move forward or sideways but never back; the coyote moves in every direction and eats a hen by jumping over it when the point behind is empty. The hens win by hemming the coyote in. It is the same hunt game as Europe’s fox and geese."],"spots":[["The coyote’s point","The coyote’s head in the central circle: where the coyote starts."],["The hens’ points","Circles with hens: where the hen pieces are set out."],["The rules","The “EXPLICACION”: pieces, moves, and how to win."],["The title","“JUEGO DEL COYOTE” — game of the coyote."],["A coyote after a hen","At lower left, a coyote goes for a hen."],["The man with the stick","At upper right, a man raises a stick to chase off a coyote."]]},"mons":{"title":"Trictrac for Mons (Bergen), 1709","artist":"Pieter Nolpe (plate and publisher)","date":"Plate 1623–53; printed c. 1709","place":"Netherlands (Amsterdam)","game":"Trictrac (a backgammon game)","medium":"Etching, engraving and letterpress verse","size":"Sheet 35.8 × 18.6 cm (plate 12.9 × 17.5 cm)","credit":"","p":["Four men play trictrac at a table. According to the museum’s record, the letters A–D mark the Duke of Marlborough, the Elector of Bavaria, the Lord of Ouwerkerk and the Duke of Villeroy. The print is a satire on the Allies’ siege and capture of Mons (Bergen in Dutch) from the French in 1709, shown as a game of tables played for the town. In the room behind (E), King Philip V of Spain is with a woman.","The plate is signed by Pieter Nolpe as maker and publisher, but Nolpe died in the early 1650s — so an old 17th-century plate was probably reprinted with the letters and a new verse added. The verse below opens by saying that the game of great lords is all tiktak and verkeer (two tables games), then mocks each player in turn."],"spots":[["The board","The trictrac board on the table: a game played for a town."],["A, Marlborough","Standing, looking down at the board — the English general of the Allies."],["B, the Elector of Bavaria","Bareheaded, slumped back in his chair."],["C, Ouwerkerk","In a plumed hat: the Dutch general."],["E, the room behind","Through the doorway, Philip V with a woman in the bedroom."],["The verse","Dutch verse printed below, mocking the players A to E."]]},"stembus":{"title":"The Ballot Box: A Political Game of the Goose","artist":"Johan Michaël Schmidt Crans (printmaker)","date":"1867","place":"Netherlands","game":"Game of the Goose (satire)","medium":"Lithograph","size":"66 × 50 cm","credit":"","p":["A satirical print in the form of a game of the goose. It came with the weekly De Nederlandsche Spectator, no. 9, 2 March 1867, together with a loose sheet of rules (the museum’s record). Portraits of the ministers of the day fill the squares.","The game starts at the Binnenhof, the parliament in The Hague, and ends in the middle at square 64, “Insulinde” — a poetic name for the Dutch East Indies — where a painted portrait of the minister Pieter Mijer hangs from the island of Java. Mijer had gone from Minister of Colonies to Governor-General of the Indies, and the appointment set off a political storm. The extra square, one more than the usual 63, is probably there for the punchline."],"spots":[["The start","The building at top left: the game begins at the Binnenhof in The Hague."],["Mijer’s portrait","Square 63: a framed portrait of Pieter Mijer, hung by ropes from the island."],["Insulinde 64","The island and the number 64 in the middle: the finish."],["The ballot box","“DE STEMBUS” (the ballot box) at lower right — the title’s box."],["The ministers","The faces of the ministers of the day look out of the squares."],["The rooster","A rooster in one square; what it mocks is not known."]]},"koorai":{"title":"Koreans at a Board Game (from Siebold’s Nippon)","artist":"James Erxleben (lithographer), after a design by ‘Toioske’","date":"1832–52","place":"Netherlands (Leiden) / people of Korea","game":"A board game (probably go)","medium":"Tinted lithograph, hand-coloured","size":"Sheet 38 × 55.5 cm","credit":"Gift of F. G. Waller","p":["A plate from NIPPON, the great work that the German-born physician Philipp Franz von Siebold published in Leiden from what he had seen at the Dutch trading post in Nagasaki (1823–30). It is marked “TAB. I” at upper right and “KOORAI” (Korea) below, with “a fisherman’s family” in Dutch and German. Erxleben drew it on the stone; the plate names the designer as “Toioske”.","Four men sit on the ground round a board; on the left a woman leads a small child, on the right a man stands with a long stick and a pouch. A broad-brimmed black hat (gat) lies in front. The board shows a grid and round stones, so it is probably go (baduk in Korean), though the record says only “a board game”."],"spots":[["The board","A grid with round stones — probably a game of go (baduk)."],["The black hat","A broad-brimmed black hat (gat) on the ground."],["Mother and child","A woman leading a child, watching the game."],["The standing man","A man standing with a long stick and a pouch."],["The man bending over","In a blue jacket, he peers at the board."],["The title","“KOORAI” (Korea), with “a fisherman’s family” in Dutch and German."]]},"cairo":{"title":"People Round a Board Game outside a Café (Cairo)","artist":"Délié & Béchard (Hippolyte Délié and Émile Béchard)","date":"c. 1869–90","place":"Egypt (Cairo)","game":"A board game (probably chess)","medium":"Albumen print","size":"10.8 × 16.4 cm","credit":"","p":["A photograph by Hippolyte Délié and Émile Béchard, French photographers who kept a studio in Cairo. The mount is printed with the studio’s name, “by the Ezbekiyya Garden”, and “Cairo (Egypt)”. Pictures like this were bought by travellers as souvenirs of Egypt.","Men sit on the ground before a shopfront with a latticed window, gathered round a board on a low stand. Tall pieces stand on the board, so it is probably chess (shatranj in Arabic), though the museum’s record says only “a board game”. Everyone holds still for the long exposure."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","The board on its low stand, with tall pieces: probably chess."],["The latticed window","A window fitted with wooden lattice, typical of old Cairo houses."],["The man with the stick","Seated with a stick, watching the game."],["The basket","A woven basket on the ground."],["The watching child","A child peers at the board from behind."],["The studio’s name","“AU JARDIN DE L’ESBÉKIEH” and “CAIRE (EGYPTE)” on the mount."]]},"vanmour":{"title":"Party of Armenians Playing Cards","artist":"Jean-Baptiste Vanmour","date":"c. 1720–37","place":"Ottoman Empire (Istanbul)","game":"Cards","medium":"Oil on canvas","size":"44.5 × 58.5 cm","credit":"","p":["Born in Flanders, Vanmour went to Istanbul in 1699 with the French ambassador and lived in the city until his death, painting court ceremonies and the daily life of its people. The museum notes that the men’s clothing shows them to be Armenians.","By the light of a large candle, men and women sit round a table spread with red cloth, playing cards. Cards lie dealt on the table, and some study the hands they hold. In the left corner an old woman is spinning. The record does not say which game it is."],"spots":[["Cards on the table","Cards laid on the red-covered table."],["The large candle","A single candle lights the whole party."],["The old woman spinning","An old woman spins in the left corner."],["A woman with her hand","At the far right, a woman studies her cards."],["The man in fur","A man in a fur-trimmed coat; the clothes mark the men as Armenians."],["Reaching across","A figure leans over the table, cards in hand."]]},"algiers":{"title":"Three Men Playing a Board Game (Algiers)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1851–1900","place":"Algeria (Algiers)","game":"A board game (probably backgammon)","medium":"Albumen print","size":"14.0 × 10.0 cm","credit":"","p":["An anonymous photograph inscribed “Alger” (Algiers) by hand below the image. With chairs and a small table set before a plain backdrop, it was probably taken in a studio. Photographs of North African people like this were bought by travellers and collectors.","Two men face each other across a rimmed board, and the man on the right is reaching for a piece. Round pieces are stacked on the board, so it is probably backgammon (known in the region as tawla and other names), though the record says only “a board game”. On the little table in front stand coffee things."],"spots":[["Board and pieces","A rimmed board with round pieces stacked on it."],["The standing onlooker","A man with a sash at his waist stands behind, looking down at the game."],["The old man, left","An older man with a wrapped headcloth, deep in thought."],["The man, right","With a white cloth over his head, he leans over the board."],["The coffee things","A tray and small cups on a fretwork side table."],["The moving hand","The right-hand player’s hand, about to take a piece."]]},"circo":{"title":"Circus Game (Juego del Circo)","artist":"Manuel Manilla (?)","date":"Undated","place":"Mexico","game":"A betting game with two dice","medium":"Relief engraving or photo relief etching on tan wove paper","size":"26.2 × 35.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A game sheet attributed, with a question mark, to the Mexican printmaker Manuel Manilla. At bottom right it names its seller, the print shop “El Teatro”, with an address. Cheap sheets like this were sold in the streets and, it seems, played on just as they were.","A circus tent sits in the middle, ringed by trick riders, a human tower, a strongman and more, each marked with a number from 2 to 12. The “Explanation” at top left says anyone, children too, may play: throw two dice and put a stake on the matching square, or take the stakes already there. Whoever throws 12 takes everything."],"spots":[["The circus tent","The title “JUEGO DEL CIRCO” and the tent, which is square 12."],["The rules","The rules, headed “ESPLICACION” (explanation)."],["The trick rider","A dancer on the back of a galloping horse: square 6."],["The human tower","One man standing on another’s shoulders: square 4."],["The strongman","A man hoisting a cannon-like weight: square 8."],["The print shop’s name","“De venta, Estampería ‘El Teatro’” and the address."]]},"loteria":{"title":"Lotería Picture Cards","artist":"José Guadalupe Posada","date":"1880–1913","place":"Mexico","game":"Lotería (a picture-calling game)","medium":"Relief engraving or photo relief etching on tan wove paper","size":"30.0 × 40.0 cm","credit":"","p":["The Mexican printmaker Posada drew a great many images for the Mexico City publisher Antonio Vanegas Arroyo, whose cheap sheets carried news, songs and games. This one bears the title “JUEGO DE LOTERIA” and Vanegas Arroyo’s name.","Thirty-eight small named pictures fill the sheet: a star, the moon, a scorpion, the devil, a skull, a rooster, a printing shop and more. In lotería, like bingo, a caller draws cards and calls out the pictures, and players mark matching pictures on their boards with beans or the like. The sheet was probably cut up into cards."],"spots":[["Title and publisher","“JUEGO DE LOTERIA” and the name of Vanegas Arroyo’s shop."],["The star","“ESTRELLA”, the first picture at top left."],["The moon","“LUNA”, a crescent with a face."],["Death","“MUERTE”: a skeleton with a scythe, playing a pipe."],["The pulque gatherer","“TLACHIQUERO”: a man collecting agave sap."],["The print shop","“IMPRENTA”: workers at a press, where sheets like this were made."]]},"jeupol":{"title":"“Jeu polonais”, or Draughts Board","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1833–1900","place":"Belgium or the Netherlands","game":"Draughts (10 × 10 board)","medium":"Woodcut with letterpress text","size":"32.2 × 39.6 cm","credit":"","p":["A single sheet titled in French and Dutch, “JEU POLONAIS OF DAMSPEL”. “Jeu polonais” (the Polish game) was the French name for draughts on a 10 × 10 board. At top right the publisher’s serial number reads “n. 106”.","As the museum’s record says, there are 100 squares, 50 white and 50 black, with ornamental borders left and right. No pieces are printed, so it was probably a cheap board played on as it was, with beans or buttons for men. The paper still shows its folds."],"spots":[["The title","The words “JEU POLONAIS OF DAMSPEL”."],["The serial number","The publisher’s number “n. 106”."],["The left border","A column of round flower motifs."],["The right border","The same pattern on the other side."],["The hundred squares","The 10 × 10 board; the dark squares are hatched with fine lines."],["The fold","A fold line left in the paper; perhaps it was carried folded."]]},"dkaart":{"title":"Dominoes and Playing Cards to Cut Out","artist":"Anonymous (Brepols)","date":"1800–1833","place":"Belgium (Turnhout)","game":"Dominoes and cards","medium":"Woodcut coloured in blue, red and yellow; letterpress text","size":"34.3 × 41.9 cm","credit":"","p":["According to the museum’s record, this sheet prints 32 playing cards and 28 dominoes to be cut out. It is number 114 in the list of the publisher Brepols, with the retailer “D. Pouwels, Bergen-op-Zoom” at the foot. The title runs in Dutch and French.","On the left, the 28 dominoes from double blank to double six are stacked in a triangle. On the right are 32 cards, seven to ten plus court cards and aces in each suit: a pack for games such as piquet. Because nobody cut it up, we can see it as it left the press."],"spots":[["The title","The words “DOMINO EN KAARTSPEL”."],["Double blank","The top of the triangle: the domino with no pips."],["Double six","The highest piece, at the right end of the bottom row."],["Ace of spades","Second card down in the left-hand column."],["A court card","A king coloured in red, blue and yellow."],["The retailer","“D. POUWELS, Bergen-op-Zoom” at the bottom."]]},"apen":{"title":"Two Monkeys Playing Trictrac","artist":"Quirin Boel, after David Teniers","date":"1635–1690","place":"Flanders","game":"Trictrac (a relative of backgammon)","medium":"Etching","size":"11.0 × 14.5 cm","credit":"","p":["One of a series, a title print and six plates, in which monkeys act out human pursuits. At lower left it reads “D. Teniers invented and published, with privilege”: Quirin Boel etched the design of the Flemish painter Teniers.","As the museum’s record describes, two monkeys sit on the floor with a trictrac board between them. The one on the left drinks wine from a glass; the one on the right, in a hat with a cock’s feather, reaches for a piece. It probably laughs at people given over to drink and gaming."],"spots":[["The board","A folding trictrac board on the floor, with its points and men."],["The left-hand monkey","A monkey in a coat, seated at the board."],["The wine glass","The glass raised by the left-hand monkey."],["The feathered hat","A cock’s feather in the right-hand monkey’s hat."],["The jug","A jug at the left-hand monkey’s feet."],["The inscription","“D Teniers in et excud cum privilegio”."]]},"dstereo":{"title":"Men Playing Dominoes at a Table (Stereograph)","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1850–1880","place":"Unknown (Dutch inscription)","game":"Dominoes","medium":"Albumen print (stereograph)","size":"8.5 × 17.0 cm","credit":"","p":["A stereograph: two almost identical photographs side by side. Seen through a viewer, a stereoscope, they merge into one scene with depth. At the left edge is written by hand “Het dominospel” (the game of dominoes).","A table has been set out in the sun before a building, and men in shirtsleeves hold their dominoes. Two stand and watch the game; the man on the right wears a white cap. As everyone had to keep still, the scene was probably arranged for the camera."],"spots":[["Tiles on the table","Dominoes held face-down in the players’ hands."],["The man in the white cap","Seated at the right, holding his tiles."],["The man with the neckerchief","Standing, looking down at the game."],["The man on the left","A young man leaning on his elbow over his tiles."],["The inscription","“Het dominospel.” written at the left edge."],["The second image","The right-hand picture, taken from a slightly shifted point: together the two look solid."]]},"gojo":{"title":"Two Women Playing Go","artist":"Anonymous","date":"1870–1891","place":"Japan","game":"Probably go","medium":"Hand-coloured albumen print","size":"9.0 × 13.5 cm","credit":"","p":["A black-and-white photograph coloured by hand with a brush. Pictures like this were made in studios in Yokohama and elsewhere as souvenirs for foreigners. This one was mounted in an album of photographs from a journey to Manila.","Before an alcove-like display and a folding screen, two women sit on either side of a footed board, and the woman on the left is placing a stone. White and black stones lie on the board, so it is probably go, though the museum’s record says “a board game”. On the tray in front are sweets and dishes."],"spots":[["Board and stones","A thick footed board with white and black stones."],["The placing hand","The left-hand woman setting down a stone."],["The woman on the left","She turns to face us; her obi is tied in a large knot."],["The woman on the right","In a striped kimono, her hand by the board."],["Tray and dishes","Sweets and cups laid out in front."],["The flowers","A vase of flowers on a stand behind."]]},"meade":{"title":"Two Men Playing Chess","artist":"Charles Richard Meade and Henry William Mathew Meade","date":"1852","place":"United States","game":"Chess","medium":"Daguerreotype","size":"6.9 × 8.3 cm","credit":"","p":["A daguerreotype from the studio of the Meade brothers, English-born photographers working in America. A daguerreotype forms its image directly on a silver-plated copper sheet, so each is unique and cannot be reprinted; this one sits in a small leather case behind a gilt mat.","Two young men face a chessboard on a little stand; the one on the left has his fingers on a piece. The one on the right, hand to his chest, studies the board. Because they had to hold still for a long time, the move is probably a posed one."],"spots":[["The chessboard","The board and white pieces on a stand draped with flowered cloth."],["The man on the left","His fingers on a piece."],["The man on the right","Looking down at the board."],["The hand on the chest","The right-hand player’s thoughtful hand."],["The table cloth","A small stand covered with flowered fabric."],["The gilt mat","The gold mat and case that protect the plate."]]}};
